\section{Keep value flow and execution conditions distinct}

A function-wide selector can see a producer and consumer even when a block boundary separates them. It can also make choices that agree across a loop backedge, where there is no bottom-up traversal of the whole def-use graph. Hjort Blindell illustrates this with a factorial function: the loop-carried values appear through \(\phi\)-nodes and yield cycles in the SSA graph \cite[chapter~5]{blindell2016instruction}. A \(\phi\) uses the value from the edge actually taken; it does not evaluate its incoming definitions in the join block. The data-only SSA graph discussed in the book is not the entire SSA IR. It omits the transfers of control needed to select branches and decide when effectful computations execute.

A program dependence graph (PDG) makes a different fact explicit: which predicate outcomes govern the execution of operations. Along with data dependencies, this can expose where moving or reordering an operation is worth considering. Control dependence is not an ordered list of blocks, however. The book's factorial example shows that some operations in different blocks share the same control condition; it also points out that emitting assembly from dependencies alone is harder when block boundaries are obscured. A selector can retain the CFG for concrete placement and use dependence information to constrain movement, rather than forcing one graph to answer both questions.

\section{Selection includes a placement obligation}

An ordinary SSA use must be dominated by its definition, with definitions preceding uses within a block. For a \(\phi\) operand, the relevant use site is the incoming edge: a value from one arm of a branch need not dominate the join block. Hjort Blindell et al.'s \emph{Complete and Practical Universal Instruction Selection} combines control and data in a universal function graph, with definition edges pinning values associated with \(\phi\)-nodes to appropriate blocks. Their representation leaves room to place other computations later; a definition edge is not a general license to move loads, stores, or traps.

Each candidate should identify permissible locations or carry constraints from which they can be derived. Check dominance, edge-specific operands and within-block order after fixing placements. Preserve dependence on memory state and other effects: an aliasing store may make a read movable only within a smaller region than its value dependencies suggest. A potentially trapping instruction cannot be speculated onto a new path merely because its inputs are available there. A pure, total instruction may sometimes be speculated legally yet should still be kept off a hot path if moving it there increases execution frequency.

Mark the backedge uses before checking order. A dependence from the body to a header \(\phi\) carries a value into the next iteration; the body does not need to precede the header in a single static schedule. Conversely, a cycle created by grouping computations that depend on each other within one iteration cannot be justified by the presence of a surrounding loop. The checker needs to preserve that distinction.

