\section{Sink an address calculation into a guarded load}

Sum \(n\) words, returning zero if the length is zero. The count \(n\) fits in a 64-bit unsigned index; the sum wraps at 32 bits. Address formation is total, modulo \(2^{64}\). Each read may fault if its address does not designate a readable word. Assume no intervening writes and a target \texttt{LDX4} with the same access and fault semantics as the plain read, using base plus four times the index.

\begin{verbatim}
entry: goto H
H: i = phi(entry:0, B:i1); s = phi(entry:0, B:s1)
   a = addr64(base, 4*i)
   if i < n goto B else goto E
B: x = read32(a); s1 = add32(s,x); i1 = i+1
   goto H
E: return s

# Anchor at read32: bind its address producer in H.
# Place LDX4 in B, where the load already executes.
n=2, words=[5,7]:
 H(i=0,s=0) -> B(x=5) -> H(i=1,s=5)
 H(i=1,s=5) -> B(x=7) -> H(i=2,s=12) -> E(12)
# Negative test: n=0 and base is not readable.
 Original and B-placement: H(i=0,s=0) -> E(0)
 H-placement before the branch: attempts a read and traps
\end{verbatim}

In the positive case, the selected instruction covers operations from two IR blocks but is emitted only in \(B\). The address computation has no other users, the header's \(i\) dominates \(B\), and combining the two operations does not create an extra read. For \(n=2\), the separately represented address expression executes three times, including on the exit test; the selected address calculation executes twice, as part of the two reads. In the negative case the availability of \(i\) in \(H\) is unchanged. It is the control condition of the read, not dominance, that distinguishes the legal placement from the illegal one.

\Cref{fig:visual-276} shows the control edge that must guard the fused instruction, even though its operands are available earlier.

