\section{An edge is also a place to do work}

After the cover is selected, PHI lowering may require copies on incoming edges. Treat them as parallel assignments: two locations that exchange values cannot be updated sequentially without a temporary or a swap. If a predecessor has multiple successors and a successor has multiple predecessors, a copy intended solely for their connecting edge generally needs edge splitting rather than unconditional insertion in either block. Recheck edge identities and instruction order after splitting. For the loop above, initialization and the backedge supply different values to both \(\phi\)-nodes; moving a backedge-only copy to entry would change the first iteration regardless of the quality of the cover. A local selector is a useful fallback, but its boundary rules must agree with global selection about which values and effects cross each edge.
